#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084464 :tutorial/content "The **expected value** (also known as the **expectation** or **mean**) of a discrete random variable is the average value we'd expect to get if we observed the random variable many times.\n\nFor a discrete random variable $X$ with probability mass function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S,$ we can calculate the expected value using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\textrm E[X] = \\sum\\limits_{x \\in S} x \\cdot f(x)\n$$\n\nIn other words, the expected value is the sum of products of each value of $X$ and its associated probability, taken over all possible values of $X.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "e1cdabf3-eea2-5288-b192-5ba380e68539" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3849 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}]}
